WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.789
This is the technical deep dive
for Book 06 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:09.074
Modernization Workbook, Data
Platform: SQL Server Identity.

4
00:00:09.074 --> 00:00:14.256
The book moves the SQL Server estate off
Windows Authentication and onto Microsoft

5
00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:18.401
Entra ID, and this session is for
the people who do that work: database

6
00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:22.028
administrators, the identity
and server teams beside them,

7
00:00:22.028 --> 00:00:24.175
and the security staff who sign off.

8
00:00:24.175 --> 00:00:25.737
It comes in two parts.

9
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:29.371
Part 1 is the strategy: why
the inheritance has to end,

10
00:00:29.371 --> 00:00:35.201
how the estate is rationalized, the
authentication decision, and how NTLM goes away.

11
00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:39.050
Part 2 walks the ten runbooks
in the order a DBA runs them.

12
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:44.885
One convention throughout: April 1, 2027
is the program's own NTLM backstop,

13
00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:50.120
not a Microsoft deadline, and I'll
say which dates are external.

14
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.544
Module 1 is the
inheritance and the estate:

15
00:00:53.544 --> 00:00:58.358
what Windows Authentication in SQL Server
really is, the drivers that end it,

16
00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:03.095
and the inventory that decides which
instances enter the migration at all.

17
00:01:03.095 --> 00:01:05.860
The book's whole order
comes out of this module.

18
00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.835
Start with what the engine actually does.

19
00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:12.422
SQL Server doesn't check the
password for a Windows login.

20
00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:17.147
It trusts the Windows token, and Windows
Authentication can't be switched off.

21
00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:20.739
Normally the client gets a
Kerberos ticket for the instance's

22
00:01:20.739 --> 00:01:24.260
service principal name, and no
password crosses the network.

23
00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.198
The table on the slide
is the one to remember.

24
00:01:27.198 --> 00:01:31.034
When the SPN is right, remote
connections use Kerberos.

25
00:01:31.034 --> 00:01:34.973
When it's missing, they
quietly fall back to NTLM.

26
00:01:34.973 --> 00:01:37.748
When it's on the wrong
account, they fail outright.

27
00:01:37.748 --> 00:01:41.989
That silent fallback is the most
consequential fault in the inheritance,

28
00:01:41.989 --> 00:01:45.396
because nothing logs which
protocol each connection used,

29
00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:49.498
and none of these paths gives
Conditional Access anything to evaluate.

30
00:01:49.498 --> 00:01:51.073
None of this was a mistake.

31
00:01:51.073 --> 00:01:55.770
It was correct engineering for a
domain-joined world that no longer exists.

32
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.870
Three drivers end the inheritance.

33
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.607
The first is NTLM itself.

34
00:02:01.607 --> 00:02:05.579
Microsoft deprecated every
version in June 2024,

35
00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:11.583
version 1 is already removed from current
Windows, and network NTLM is next.

36
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:16.018
So the program set its
own backstop: on April 1,

37
00:02:16.018 --> 00:02:19.733
2027, it blocks NTLM across the estate.

38
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:24.759
The second driver is zero trust:
Conditional Access only acts on an

39
00:02:24.759 --> 00:02:29.695
Entra token request, and a Kerberos
or NTLM exchange never asks it.

40
00:02:29.695 --> 00:02:33.500
The third is the engine's own
identity, which needs an Entra anchor,

41
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.107
and on premises that
comes through Azure Arc.

42
00:02:36.107 --> 00:02:40.023
The dates that are really due
in 2027 are lifecycle dates for

43
00:02:40.023 --> 00:02:44.095
older Windows and SQL Server
versions, and the slide lists them.

44
00:02:44.095 --> 00:02:47.460
Those versions become
consolidation candidates.

45
00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.879
Before any authentication work,
the estate has to be counted,

46
00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:55.035
because a twenty-year estate
doesn't know what it has.

47
00:02:55.035 --> 00:03:00.047
The instances that matter most are the ones
nobody manages, so they're discovered,

48
00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:04.460
not pulled from a list, using three
instruments that each catch what the

49
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:09.248
others miss: a network sweep, the SPN
trail, and Microsoft's assessment tooling.

50
00:03:09.248 --> 00:03:10.785
Then three lenses.

51
00:03:10.785 --> 00:03:12.845
Utilization finds the zombies,

52
00:03:12.845 --> 00:03:16.410
and retiring them is the
fastest way to shrink the work.

53
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:21.810
Version matters, because Entra logins
need SQL Server 2022 or later.

54
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.720
And dependencies, like linked
servers and that quarterly

55
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.385
cross-database query, most
often overturn a naive plan.

56
00:03:29.385 --> 00:03:34.148
Each instance then gets one verdict:
retain, consolidate or retire.

57
00:03:34.148 --> 00:03:38.930
That way, authentication is
migrated once, in its final shape.

58
00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.077
Module 2 is the audit, the
principals and the decision:

59
00:03:43.077 --> 00:03:46.243
what the audit reads on
every surviving instance,

60
00:03:46.243 --> 00:03:50.875
the three Arc-eligibility categories,
the principal types that replace

61
00:03:50.875 --> 00:03:55.790
Windows and SQL logins, and the parallel
run that makes the cutover safe.

62
00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.998
The audit runs only on what the gate kept,

63
00:03:58.998 --> 00:04:03.915
and its deliverable is a compliance record
for each instance, not a spreadsheet.

64
00:04:03.915 --> 00:04:09.090
It checks the authentication mode, both in
the registry and on the running instance,

65
00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.540
because a mode change only
takes effect after a restart.

66
00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.015
It classifies every login by type.

67
00:04:15.015 --> 00:04:19.620
One trap here: the type letters are the
view's own values, and a query written

68
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.603
against a type that doesn't exist returns
zero rows and reports a clean instance.

69
00:04:24.603 --> 00:04:27.403
Active SQL logins are a critical finding,

70
00:04:27.403 --> 00:04:31.603
and an enabled SA with an old
password is the top finding of all.

71
00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:35.840
Each run is compared with the
last, and drift is a violation.

72
00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.715
The audit's most important output
is a category for every instance.

73
00:04:40.715 --> 00:04:46.039
Category 1 is ready now: Arc
connected, the SQL extension deployed,

74
00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:50.377
Entra authentication on, and
SQL Server 2022 or later.

75
00:04:50.377 --> 00:04:53.458
Category 2 is eligible
but not yet enabled,

76
00:04:53.458 --> 00:04:56.978
and that's a server action,
not a SQL Server change.

77
00:04:56.978 --> 00:05:00.278
Category 3 isn't eligible
yet: older engines,

78
00:05:00.278 --> 00:05:03.853
and failover cluster
instances on any version.

79
00:05:03.853 --> 00:05:07.571
They upgrade, or carry an
exception with a sunset date,

80
00:05:07.571 --> 00:05:11.290
and an exception doesn't save
them from the NTLM block.

81
00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.890
Then the book's most
common misunderstanding.

82
00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:17.096
Layer 1 is the engine
accepting Entra tokens,

83
00:05:17.096 --> 00:05:21.515
and a domain-joined Windows Server
2019 host can do that today.

84
00:05:21.515 --> 00:05:26.502
Layer 2 is administrators signing in
to the server itself with Entra ID.

85
00:05:26.502 --> 00:05:31.060
That needs Windows Server 2025,
and it's a separate program.

86
00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:35.303
This is where the engine
stops accepting Kerberos

87
00:05:35.303 --> 00:05:38.797
and NTLM and starts accepting
Entra-issued tokens.

88
00:05:38.797 --> 00:05:42.832
On SQL Server 2022 and later,
you create a passwordless login

89
00:05:42.832 --> 00:05:46.947
from the external provider, and
the engine maps the token to it.

90
00:05:46.947 --> 00:05:52.015
Two practical points: the instance's identity
needs Graph read permission or login

91
00:05:52.015 --> 00:05:55.686
creation fails, and Entra
connections are always encrypted,

92
00:05:55.686 --> 00:05:58.036
so clients must trust the certificate.

93
00:05:58.036 --> 00:05:59.360
There are three types.

94
00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.938
An Entra user login, where
Conditional Access enforces MFA

95
00:06:03.938 --> 00:06:08.235
before the token is even issued,
with no SQL-level bypass.

96
00:06:08.235 --> 00:06:13.239
An Entra group login, the familiar AD
group pattern; with a dynamic group,

97
00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:17.310
people gain and lose access as
their HR attributes change.

98
00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.902
And an application login, for a
managed identity or service principal,

99
00:06:21.902 --> 00:06:25.270
replacing a SQL login
and its stored password.

100
00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.108
No Windows login is dropped until
its Entra replacement is proven.

101
00:06:30.108 --> 00:06:32.445
The parallel run has three steps.

102
00:06:32.445 --> 00:06:37.262
Create the Entra login, after confirming
the person or group really maps to it,

103
00:06:37.262 --> 00:06:39.670
which is the most labor-intensive part.

104
00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:41.833
Validate it with the real credential.

105
00:06:41.833 --> 00:06:46.171
Then disable the old login, don't
drop it, and watch for thirty days.

106
00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:51.745
And watching means evidence, not silence:
only auditing shows what's actually used.

107
00:06:51.745 --> 00:06:56.620
Because the old login is only disabled,
a wrong mapping locks no one out.

108
00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.846
Around that pattern sit five decisions, on
the slide: consolidation gates migration,

109
00:07:01.846 --> 00:07:05.802
the parallel run is required,
SQL Authentication is eliminated,

110
00:07:05.802 --> 00:07:09.610
older and clustered instances
get a path or a dated exception,

111
00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.190
and the per-instance record
is how you prove it's done.

112
00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:19.058
Module 3 finishes the strategy: the four
connection patterns that generate NTLM,

113
00:07:19.058 --> 00:07:24.023
the three phases that remove it, how
applications upstream of the database are

114
00:07:24.023 --> 00:07:29.430
classified, and the closure condition,
which has to hold at once and keep holding.

115
00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:35.105
NTLM is a protocol, not a setting,
so no single switch removes it.

116
00:07:35.105 --> 00:07:39.730
You have to name every connection pattern
that produces it, and the book finds four.

117
00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.634
The most common is a missing or wrong SPN:

118
00:07:42.634 --> 00:07:46.505
the client can't get a Kerberos
ticket, so it falls back.

119
00:07:46.505 --> 00:07:48.379
Second, cross-site connections,

120
00:07:48.379 --> 00:07:51.768
where the client can't reach
the right domain controller.

121
00:07:51.768 --> 00:07:54.655
Check with klist before
you touch firewall rules.

122
00:07:54.655 --> 00:07:59.425
Third, linked-server passthrough,
which is a double hop NTLM can't make,

123
00:07:59.425 --> 00:08:04.530
so each definition moves to a mapped
login or an Entra or managed identity.

124
00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:09.478
Fourth, clients with no domain ticket,
like an application on an unjoined host,

125
00:08:09.478 --> 00:08:12.030
which moves to a managed identity first.

126
00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:18.140
And NTLM version 1 found anywhere is a
critical finding, not a migration item.

127
00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.932
The program removes NTLM in three phases,

128
00:08:21.932 --> 00:08:25.153
and it's worth knowing
why the order matters.

129
00:08:25.153 --> 00:08:29.655
After the April 2027 block,
any connection that needs NTLM

130
00:08:29.655 --> 00:08:33.877
fails in Windows itself, before
the engine ever sees it.

131
00:08:33.877 --> 00:08:38.390
So every pattern has to be fixed
before the last phase, not after.

132
00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:40.866
Phase A discovers, using the audit and the

133
00:08:40.866 --> 00:08:43.415
Windows logs to show what really happened.

134
00:08:43.415 --> 00:08:46.603
Phase B restricts, and
here precision matters.

135
00:08:46.603 --> 00:08:51.036
The first setting blocks only the
oldest versions, not NTLM as a whole,

136
00:08:51.036 --> 00:08:56.014
and the deny settings for version 2 have
no exception list for the server role.

137
00:08:56.014 --> 00:08:57.628
Then comes the pattern work.

138
00:08:57.628 --> 00:09:00.266
Phase C blocks NTLM on every host,

139
00:09:00.266 --> 00:09:04.566
which is safe only after
login migration is complete.

140
00:09:04.566 --> 00:09:09.880
The book leaves one decision open: whether
the date holds for the older long tail.

141
00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.268
The database is only half of the path.

142
00:09:13.268 --> 00:09:16.894
An application that moved
user sign-in to Entra ID but

143
00:09:16.894 --> 00:09:20.768
still reaches SQL Server with
a SQL login hasn't finished.

144
00:09:20.768 --> 00:09:24.770
Both legs, user to application
and application to database,

145
00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.893
have to leave Active Directory.

146
00:09:26.893 --> 00:09:29.854
For the user leg, the
program sorts applications

147
00:09:29.854 --> 00:09:32.667
into four classes in
strict preference order.

148
00:09:32.667 --> 00:09:36.068
OpenID Connect is the
destination; SAML is next;

149
00:09:36.068 --> 00:09:41.425
Application Proxy fronts an application
that can't change; and Entra Domain

150
00:09:41.425 --> 00:09:46.867
Services is the last resort, which still
uses NTLM and needs a dated exception.

151
00:09:46.867 --> 00:09:51.447
The database leg is a second axis,
set by evidence: can the driver and

152
00:09:51.447 --> 00:09:55.943
the vendor support Entra, and is it
a config change or a code change?

153
00:09:55.943 --> 00:10:00.510
Together they tell you what moves now
and what gets scheduled or excepted.

154
00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:05.644
A transformation that reaches its end
state and stops watching will slide back,

155
00:10:05.644 --> 00:10:10.360
because any administrator with permission
can switch an instance back to mixed mode.

156
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.898
So closure is a posture, not a date.

157
00:10:12.898 --> 00:10:17.784
It holds when five conditions are true
at once on every production instance:

158
00:10:17.784 --> 00:10:22.127
no usable Windows logins apart from
a documented break-glass group,

159
00:10:22.127 --> 00:10:24.221
SQL Authentication eliminated,

160
00:10:24.221 --> 00:10:29.185
Entra authentication observed working
and nothing else, the engine holding

161
00:10:29.185 --> 00:10:33.761
a cloud identity, and every human
using MFA through Conditional Access.

162
00:10:33.761 --> 00:10:36.989
No single setting does this,
which is why the book names

163
00:10:36.989 --> 00:10:40.648
the combined state the
Entra-exclusive operational posture.

164
00:10:40.648 --> 00:10:45.861
And drift, like a new SQL login or an
offline Arc agent, is a violation.

165
00:10:45.861 --> 00:10:51.430
The final months before April 2027
are for validation, not migration.

166
00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.055
Part 2 is the runbooks.

167
00:10:54.055 --> 00:10:59.330
Module 4 covers the first five, from
prerequisites to login migration.

168
00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:03.406
One rule runs through all ten:
anything that changes state plans

169
00:11:03.406 --> 00:11:07.867
first or writes a reviewable script,
then goes through change control.

170
00:11:07.867 --> 00:11:11.560
The commands are illustrative,
so run them in test first.

171
00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.585
Runbook R01 changes nothing in production.

172
00:11:15.585 --> 00:11:17.598
It sets up what the other nine assume.

173
00:11:17.598 --> 00:11:19.853
First, the toolchain: PowerShell 7,

174
00:11:19.853 --> 00:11:25.491
a plain Python interpreter for computing
integrity seals, and the program's modules,

175
00:11:25.491 --> 00:11:29.760
with live Azure and Graph calls
behind an explicit opt-in switch.

176
00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.722
Second, the evidence model.

177
00:11:31.722 --> 00:11:36.628
Every function emits a sealed envelope
with its records, counts and provenance,

178
00:11:36.628 --> 00:11:41.609
and the seal leaves out the timestamp, so
identical inputs give an identical hash.

179
00:11:41.609 --> 00:11:46.448
That makes every artifact reproducible,
and runs comparable over time.

180
00:11:46.448 --> 00:11:50.237
Third, the safety model:
read-only functions run directly,

181
00:11:50.237 --> 00:11:55.073
and anything that changes state plans
by default or only writes a script.

182
00:11:55.073 --> 00:11:58.148
Fourth, every function
accepts captured snapshots,

183
00:11:58.148 --> 00:12:02.170
so you can test the whole chain
without touching a live estate.

184
00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:07.720
Runbook R02 is count everything
first, decide what survives second.

185
00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:12.144
The program's scripts find instances
from their SPNs and a target list,

186
00:12:12.144 --> 00:12:14.509
but they miss anything without an SPN,

187
00:12:14.509 --> 00:12:19.620
like an Express instance an installer
quietly dropped on an application server.

188
00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.908
So cast a wider net with
the other discovery tools.

189
00:12:22.908 --> 00:12:27.672
The inventory function then merges
every source, collapses duplicates,

190
00:12:27.672 --> 00:12:32.038
records which tool found each
instance, and makes a first-pass call:

191
00:12:32.038 --> 00:12:37.358
long unused means retire, older engines
consolidate, and newer ones are retained.

192
00:12:37.358 --> 00:12:41.957
Anything unclear lands in
review, never silently in retain.

193
00:12:41.957 --> 00:12:46.223
One caution: usage statistics
reset whenever the engine restarts,

194
00:12:46.223 --> 00:12:48.395
so read the start time beside them.

195
00:12:48.395 --> 00:12:53.182
Architects confirm or override each
verdict, with the reason recorded.

196
00:12:53.182 --> 00:12:56.910
And warn security operations
before network sweeps.

197
00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.948
Runbook R03 is the audit script itself.

198
00:13:00.948 --> 00:13:04.572
It changes nothing, so run
it first and run it often.

199
00:13:04.572 --> 00:13:07.839
Watch one trap: an
under-privileged audit account

200
00:13:07.839 --> 00:13:10.786
sees only part of the
login list, with no error.

201
00:13:10.786 --> 00:13:13.880
So grant the narrowest read
that sees every login,

202
00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:16.673
and check the count
against a known instance.

203
00:13:16.673 --> 00:13:20.972
It runs in three passes, from
discovery only up to the deep audit,

204
00:13:20.972 --> 00:13:24.197
and produces a readiness
verdict per instance,

205
00:13:24.197 --> 00:13:28.910
flagging blockers like mixed mode,
an enabled SA and orphaned logins.

206
00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:31.520
It doesn't spot failover
cluster instances,

207
00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:33.985
so flag those from the cluster inventory.

208
00:13:33.985 --> 00:13:37.173
The slide notes two input
defects to work around.

209
00:13:37.173 --> 00:13:39.659
And the script degrades
rather than crashing.

210
00:13:39.659 --> 00:13:42.574
Keep every run under version control,

211
00:13:42.574 --> 00:13:46.510
because it's the raw input
to monitoring in R09.

212
00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:50.710
Runbook R04 gives the
host its cloud identity.

213
00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:54.517
Connecting a server to Arc
creates a managed identity with no

214
00:13:54.517 --> 00:13:58.473
secret anyone has to manage, and
without it there's no Entra login.

215
00:13:58.473 --> 00:14:00.684
The prerequisites carry the traps.

216
00:14:00.684 --> 00:14:03.999
In a GCC-Moderate tenant,
the Arc resources live

217
00:14:03.999 --> 00:14:07.398
in a commercial subscription
in the same tenant.

218
00:14:07.398 --> 00:14:11.966
And the engine contacts Entra ID
directly, not through the agent's proxy,

219
00:14:11.966 --> 00:14:16.998
which is the one people miss: the agent
shows Connected while Entra logins fail.

220
00:14:16.998 --> 00:14:21.844
Then four steps: enroll, validate
the token, confirm the extension,

221
00:14:21.844 --> 00:14:25.523
and turn on Entra
authentication, on a pilot first.

222
00:14:25.523 --> 00:14:29.410
Two program functions need correcting
before you run them for real.

223
00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:33.383
And rollback has a catch:
reconnecting creates a new identity,

224
00:14:33.383 --> 00:14:36.160
so permissions must be granted again.

225
00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.767
Runbook R05 adds the Entra
login beside the Windows one,

226
00:14:40.767 --> 00:14:44.960
watches both for thirty days,
and disables rather than drops.

227
00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:49.247
Don't start until the instance
is on SQL Server 2022 or later,

228
00:14:49.247 --> 00:14:52.747
connected to Arc, and the
estate is consolidated.

229
00:14:52.747 --> 00:14:57.984
The one piece of real judgment is mapping
each Windows login to an Entra principal.

230
00:14:57.984 --> 00:15:01.642
The program builds that from
the identity reconciliation,

231
00:15:01.642 --> 00:15:05.673
not a spreadsheet, prefers groups,
and flags anything unresolved.

232
00:15:05.673 --> 00:15:09.360
Then create matching database
users with the same permissions.

233
00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.175
During the thirty days, capture
logins with SQL Server Audit,

234
00:15:13.175 --> 00:15:16.685
because a point-in-time query
misses short connections.

235
00:15:16.685 --> 00:15:20.948
You exit when every application
also shows up on its Entra login.

236
00:15:20.948 --> 00:15:25.722
Cutover is disabling the old login,
so rollback is just re-enabling it.

237
00:15:25.722 --> 00:15:28.110
And close an enabled SA here.

238
00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:34.029
Module 5 covers the last five runbooks:
NTLM, groups and the Conditional

239
00:15:34.029 --> 00:15:39.460
Access perimeter, applications,
continuous monitoring, and certificates.

240
00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:44.710
Detection stays read-only, and every change
is dry-run first through change control.

241
00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:49.100
By the end, the posture is
provable on every scheduled run.

242
00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:52.604
Runbook R06 is detect, then remediate,

243
00:15:52.604 --> 00:15:58.200
and most of what you find is a missing or
misplaced name, not a protocol choice.

244
00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.956
In the program's experience,
a missing SPN is the most

245
00:16:01.956 --> 00:16:06.062
common cause of silent NTLM,
and a duplicate is the second.

246
00:16:06.062 --> 00:16:11.562
Three read-only audits map the SPNs, the
collisions and the delegation settings.

247
00:16:11.562 --> 00:16:16.587
Remediation changes production, so the
tools only produce reviewable plans.

248
00:16:16.587 --> 00:16:21.299
Register missing SPNs, leave each
one with exactly one enabled owner,

249
00:16:21.299 --> 00:16:24.357
and move linked servers
to Entra or Kerberos,

250
00:16:24.357 --> 00:16:28.076
with a stored password only
as a documented exception.

251
00:16:28.076 --> 00:16:31.630
The Group Policy steps tighten in stages,

252
00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:34.975
with the full block
last, on April 1, 2027.

253
00:16:34.975 --> 00:16:39.012
Verify from a remote client that
the connection reads Kerberos.

254
00:16:39.012 --> 00:16:44.630
Because the block comes last, the fixes
are proven long before anything is refused.

255
00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:49.117
Runbook R07 moves the
access-control layer.

256
00:16:49.117 --> 00:16:50.818
The group model survives.

257
00:16:50.818 --> 00:16:54.799
What changes is who maintains
membership, who can administer it,

258
00:16:54.799 --> 00:16:57.429
and the boundary every
sign-in has to cross.

259
00:16:57.429 --> 00:17:02.642
Phase 1 is read-only: export the groups
and the Conditional Access policies,

260
00:17:02.642 --> 00:17:08.359
and classify every group into one of four
types, from organizational unit (OU)-based

261
00:17:08.359 --> 00:17:13.655
groups that become dynamic, to distribution
lists that never get a SQL login.

262
00:17:13.655 --> 00:17:15.380
Phase 2 has a fixed order.

263
00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:19.223
Create the DBA administrative
unit with restricted management

264
00:17:19.223 --> 00:17:22.568
turned on at creation, because
that can't be changed later.

265
00:17:22.568 --> 00:17:27.255
Then scope the DBA lead's admin role
to it through PIM, and add the groups.

266
00:17:27.255 --> 00:17:32.222
The payoff: once every login is an Entra
principal, Conditional Access becomes

267
00:17:32.222 --> 00:17:37.090
the perimeter for human access, which
the pilot needs to confirm for Arc.

268
00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.503
Runbook R08 is the last mile.

269
00:17:40.503 --> 00:17:45.320
An application that signs users in with
Entra ID but reaches the database with

270
00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:49.990
a stored password has moved its front
door and left the back door where it was.

271
00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:53.732
Phase 1 inventories which
applications still bind to LDAP,

272
00:17:53.732 --> 00:17:57.699
using domain controller logs, and
joins that to the audit so each

273
00:17:57.699 --> 00:18:01.740
application gets a class and a
planned connection-string change.

274
00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:05.215
Phase 2 is the simplest
change and the most gated.

275
00:18:05.215 --> 00:18:08.890
It's made in application
configuration, never by a script.

276
00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:13.390
In production, name a specific
authentication mode rather than the default,

277
00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:16.890
which tries providers in sequence
and can switch identity.

278
00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:21.179
Three gates come before cutover: the
login exists, it has the right role,

279
00:18:21.179 --> 00:18:24.802
and the change was tested on
a production-equivalent stack.

280
00:18:24.802 --> 00:18:27.460
Rollback is the old connection string.

281
00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:31.335
Runbook R09 keeps proving the posture.

282
00:18:31.335 --> 00:18:33.596
Closure isn't a day on the calendar;

283
00:18:33.596 --> 00:18:38.523
it's a record that keeps coming back
clean, and everything here is read-only.

284
00:18:38.523 --> 00:18:40.577
The deep audit runs on a schedule,

285
00:18:40.577 --> 00:18:45.294
and each run is projected onto the
per-instance closure record, which comes

286
00:18:45.294 --> 00:18:49.935
out closed, closed with an exception,
or open, with the failing fields named.

287
00:18:49.935 --> 00:18:53.387
The difference between two
runs becomes drift events,

288
00:18:53.387 --> 00:18:57.222
like mixed mode turned back on
or the Arc agent going offline.

289
00:18:57.222 --> 00:19:02.060
One caution: a registry change is
invisible until the service restarts.

290
00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:04.796
On top of that sits a behavioral layer,

291
00:19:04.796 --> 00:19:09.210
where any NTLM or Kerberos
connection is an immediate alert.

292
00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:14.197
When six evidence sets hold together
across the validation period,

293
00:19:14.197 --> 00:19:19.560
the transformation is closed, and
April 1, 2027 arrives as a non-event.

294
00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:23.547
Runbook R10 is discovery,
not provisioning.

295
00:19:23.547 --> 00:19:27.398
Before anyone sets a date to switch
off the certificate authority,

296
00:19:27.398 --> 00:19:29.848
someone has to find out
what it still signs.

297
00:19:29.848 --> 00:19:34.125
The program hasn't decided yet on
moving from AD CS to a cloud PKI,

298
00:19:34.125 --> 00:19:38.073
so this runbook gathers the
evidence that decision needs.

299
00:19:38.073 --> 00:19:42.192
A read-only audit records the
issuing CA on every certificate,

300
00:19:42.192 --> 00:19:44.535
with a few checks inside the engine.

301
00:19:44.535 --> 00:19:46.273
Then three dependencies.

302
00:19:46.273 --> 00:19:48.935
The engine's TLS certificate matters most,

303
00:19:48.935 --> 00:19:52.360
because every Entra connection
requires encryption.

304
00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:56.260
Certificate-mapped logins carry
less risk than the source claimed.

305
00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:58.365
And certificate-based sign-in,

306
00:19:58.365 --> 00:20:03.247
the interim answer for the NTLM long
tail, needs a replacement CA first.

307
00:20:03.247 --> 00:20:05.762
The finding comes down to one question:

308
00:20:05.762 --> 00:20:08.984
does AD CS retire before
its replacement is ready?

309
00:20:08.984 --> 00:20:13.140
If so, the interim posture is
lost exactly when it's needed.

310
00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:16.840
To close, five things to
take back to the estate.

311
00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:20.053
Count before you migrate,
because migrating an instance

312
00:20:20.053 --> 00:20:23.402
you should have retired is wasted
work against a fixed date.

313
00:20:23.402 --> 00:20:29.877
Measure, don't assume, because a missing
SPN turns Kerberos into NTLM silently.

314
00:20:29.877 --> 00:20:36.290
Keep it reversible: create, validate,
disable, observe for thirty days, then drop.

315
00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:39.484
Finish the chain, because an
instance isn't closed while

316
00:20:39.484 --> 00:20:42.678
an application still reaches
it with a stored password.

317
00:20:42.678 --> 00:20:47.427
And prove it continuously, so done is a
queryable posture rather than a claim.

318
00:20:47.427 --> 00:20:51.377
April 1, 2027 is the program's backstop.

319
00:20:51.377 --> 00:20:53.669
If the work is finished well before it,

320
00:20:53.669 --> 00:20:58.178
and the evidence has been clean for
months, the block arrives as a non-event.

321
00:20:58.178 --> 00:21:04.615
Book 06 has the chapters and all ten
runbooks, with every external claim footnoted.

322
00:21:04.615 --> 00:21:05.440
Thank you.
